incumbency": 0.0, "money": 0.05}, "rating_margin": -8.11, "sigma_race": 6.5, "sigma_total": 7.33, "baseline_margin": -3.94, "analyst_adjustment": -0.06, "key_factors": ["District's Republican lean (R+7 PVI) in an open seat", "Nearly all expert raters call it Likely R, two say Lean R", "Democratic-sponsored polls show a near-tie, but they are older and low-quality", "Heavy outside spending from Trump-linked and Beshear-linked PACs signals competitiveness"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a Republican edge: the seat leans R+7, raters overwhelmingly call it Likely R, and markets price Alvarado around 73%. Democratic-sponsored polls from August and July show a dead heat, which justifies shading the margin slightly tighter than the ratings consensus, but their partisan sponsorship and thin sample weight argue against moving further.", "watch": "A fresh independent poll confirming the August near-tie, or sustained PAC spending on the affordability message, could shift this toward a true tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Andy Barr (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Kentucky's 6th District is an open seat with Andy Barr leaving to run for Senate, and Republicans hold a clear structural edge in a district that leaned R+7 at the presidential level. Nearly every professional rating, from Cook to Sabato, calls the race Likely Republican, with two outlets going a notch closer. Prediction markets give the Republican roughly a three-in-four chance.\n\nStill, there are signs of a real contest. Democratic-sponsored polls from July and August showed the race tied or within a point, national Democrats are spending here for the first time in eight years, and outside groups tied to Trump and Governor Beshear are running ads. Democratic nominee Zach Dembo has kept pace financially with Republican Ralph Alvarado. The evidence points to a Republican favorite, but not a safe one.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.2515, "margin": -3.92}, {